### Q1 — REFUTED for the stated correction. The exact sum gives the larger factor directly.

- **PROVED.** With the standard Hurwitz normalization,
  \[
  S_N=\sum_{t^2<4N}H(4N-t^2)
  =2\sigma(N)-\sum_{d\mid N}\min(d,N/d)
  =2\sigma(N)-2(1+p)=2(N+q).
  \]
  Thus \(S_N\bmod 2^{j+1}\) gives \(q\bmod 2^j\). The correction itself contains \(p\). [Kronecker–Hurwitz relation](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00209-020-02572-y)
- **PROVED.** Already \(\sigma(N)\bmod8=4\,\mathbf1_{p\equiv q\equiv1\bmod4}\). On \(N\equiv1\bmod4\), a native evaluator would distinguish Blum from pseudo-Blum integers, exactly Seurin’s \(2\)-\(\Phi/4\)-hiding problem. At precision \(2^k>2N\), it would factor \(N\) outright. [Seurin](https://eprint.iacr.org/2013/256.pdf)
- **PROVED.** Write \(e=\sum_{i\in I}2^i\). Then \(a_N(\Delta^e)=a_1(T_N\Delta^e)\) is the parity of the tuples \((m_i)_{i\in I}\) of **positive odd** integers satisfying \(N=\sum_{i\in I}2^i m_i^2\). Each coordinate is indexed by its binary digit. This follows from \(\Delta(X)=\sum_{m>0,\ m\ {\rm odd}}X^{m^2}\) over \(\mathbf F_2\). [Nicolas–Serre](https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.1036)
- **PROVED.** A fixed example satisfying **(b)** is
  \[
  a_N(\Delta^9)=\mathbf1_{p\equiv q\equiv3\bmod8}.
  \]
  Indeed, \(\Delta^9=m(2,0)\) extracts the \(x^2\)-coefficient of \(T_pT_q\), and \([x]T_\ell=\mathbf1_{\ell\equiv3\bmod8}\). For every \(j\ge3\), Dirichlet gives prime pairs in classes \(3,3^{-1}\bmod2^j\) and in classes \(1,1\bmod2^j\). Both products are \(1\bmod2^j\), with opposite coefficients. The counts for \(e=1,3,5\) are natively zero on these squarefree semiprimes. [Nicolas–Serre, §§6–7](https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.1039)
- **KNOWN (literature status).** I found no fixed level-one coefficient satisfying both **(a)** and **(b)** on general unfactored semiprimes, and no native formula for \(\sigma(N)\) at growing precision. The fast level-one Hecke algorithm takes the factorization of the index as input. Bach–Charles prove a factoring reduction for **exact** eigenform coefficients on most RSA moduli. Their theorem leaves the single-bit question open. [Edixhoven et al., Ch. 0](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0605244) · [Bach–Charles](https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.1192)
- **PROVED.** If \(e\) may depend on \(N\), both conditions hold literally. Take the largest \(e\le N\) congruent to \(1\bmod6\). Since \(0\le N-e<6\) and \(\Delta^e=X^e(1+O(X^8))\), the bit is \(\mathbf1_{N\equiv1\bmod6}\). It reads a public congruence.
- **PROVED.** “Some space” also has a nonconstant native trace. Take the span of \(f(z),f(5z)\) at level 10, with \(f=2E_2(2z)-E_2(z)\). For \(5\nmid N\) the trace is \(2\sigma(N)\equiv0\bmod8\). For \(N=5s\) it is \(6(1+s)\bmod8\), since \(U_5\) has trace \(6\) on this span. The only factor this detects is the public level prime. [Stein, Eisenstein series and Hecke operators](https://www.williamstein.org/books/modform/stein-modform.pdf)

### Q2 — KNOWN (open). No general classical polynomial-time or sub-\(L_N[1/3]\) factoring algorithm is known with a QR decision oracle.

- **PROVED.** Let \(J\) be the Jacobi character. On \(J(x)=+1\), QR gives \(\chi_p(x)=\chi_q(x)\). Choose a unit \(u\) with \(J(u)=-1\) by random sampling. On \(J(x)=-1\), query \(xu\) and guess \(\chi_p(u)\). The two guesses give exactly the two globally consistent characters \(\chi_p,\chi_q\). Run a proposed smaller-prime decoder with both and verify its outputs. Conversely, \(\chi_p\) and \(J\) decide QR.
- **KNOWN.** Goldwasser–Micali use decisional QR. Factoring with that oracle is the **Gap–Rabin** problem. Jager–Schwenk’s factoring equivalence concerns implementations of QR by *generic ring algorithms*. Its proof requires access to the implementation’s ring operations. [Goldwasser–Micali](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0022000084900709) · [Gap–Rabin, §3.3](https://www.di.ens.fr/users/pointche/Documents/Papers/2001_pkc.pdf) · [Jager–Schwenk](https://www.iacr.org/archive/asiacrypt2009/59120396/59120396.pdf)
- **PROVED under GRH.** The small-prime fingerprint distinguishes candidate primes \(p,t<\sqrt N\). For \(p\ne t\), \(\chi_p\chi_t\) is nonprincipal modulo \(pt<N\). A small prime dividing \(pt\) distinguishes them by a zero. Otherwise the GRH least-prime-outside-a-subgroup bound gives a distinguishing \(r\le(\log N)^2\) for sufficiently large \(N\). I found no efficient decoder from these bits. Boneh–Venkatesan decode approximate modular products with known modulus. Damgård studies shifted Legendre sequences. [Lamzouri–Li–Soundararajan, Thm. 1.1](https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.3595) · [Boneh–Venkatesan](https://crypto.stanford.edu/~dabo/pubs/abstracts/dhmsb.html) · [Damgård](https://pure.au.dk/portal/en/publications/on-the-randomness-of-legendre-and-jacobi-sequences%28c1c42c90-ca39-11df-8cb9-000ea68e967b%29.html)
- **KNOWN.** Hittmeir’s *rational quartic symbol* decides QR for \(N\equiv3\bmod4\). His hierarchy of rational \(2^k\)-symbols recovers the unordered \(v_2(p-1),v_2(q-1)\) in randomized polynomial time. He explicitly leaves factoring from the hierarchy open. For ordinary fourth-power **membership**, on Blum integers fourth powers equal squares at both primes. [Hittmeir, Thms. 1.3–1.5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1604.07791)
- **PROVED.** With a public root \(s^2=a\bmod N\), QR on the corresponding split component is ordinary QR of \(x+ys\bmod N\). At a *hidden* prime and chosen root \(s\bmod p\), the bit is \(\chi_p(x+ys)\). A Rédei symbol \([a,b,p]\) is a particular Artin bit with local admissibility conditions. I found no classical factoring reduction at either requested bound from the hidden-component decision oracle. [Stevenhagen, §§5–7](https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.06250)

### Q3 — REFUTED for the two specified towers as a general decoder modulo 32. The growing public-twist question remains open.

- **PROVED by certified computation.** Take \(N_1=367\cdot751\) and \(N_2=383\cdot991\). Both are \(161\bmod256\). For **both** inputs the full 2-primary groups at \((-4N,-8N)\) are respectively \((C_{16}\times C_2,\ C_8\times C_2)\). Yet both factors are \(15\bmod32\) in the first case and \(31\bmod32\) in the second. Thus even all ranks of these two groups, together with \(N\bmod256\), lose the next bit in this cell. [PARI verification with `bnfcertify`](/Volumes/git/fc-redei/codex/scratch/redei_1_c/check.gp)
- **KNOWN.** Cyclotomic information does occur in Rédei symbols. For \(p\equiv1\bmod8\), the additive symbol \([2,2,p]=(p-1)/8\bmod2\) distinguishes \(1\) from \(9\bmod16\). It comes from a quartic character of conductor 16. [Stevenhagen, §7, especially (35)–(39)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.06250)
- **PROVED.** There is an obstruction for **any finite list of ordinary symbols** \([a_i,b_i,p]\) with slots fixed independently of \(p\). Their governing fields are dihedral of order eight or cyclic of order at most four. Their compositum, enlarged by the quadratic admissibility fields and \(\mathbf Q(\zeta_{16})\), has Galois exponent at most four. It cannot contain \(\mathbf Q(\zeta_{32})\), whose Galois group has exponent eight. Chebotarev therefore gives primes splitting completely in that compositum in **both** classes \(1,17\bmod32\). Every listed symbol agrees. [Stevenhagen, §§5–7](https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.06250)
- **KNOWN (scope).** Koymans–Milović prove spin equidistribution for the 16-rank of \(\mathbf Q(\sqrt{-2p})\). Their nonexistence theorem for a governing field for \(\mathrm{Cl}(-4p)\) assumes a short character-sum conjecture. Neither result rules out extracting cyclotomic bits jointly from varying \(\mathrm{Cl}(-dN)\). I found no uniform decoder reaching \(j\sim\frac14\log_2N\) for balanced factors. [Friedlander–Iwaniec–Mazur–Rubin](https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.6331) · [Koymans–Milović on \(-8p\)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.05505) · [conditional nonexistence, Thm. 3](https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.09597) · [Milović on the \(dpq\) families](https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.08034)

### Q4 — REFUTED. A native bounded norm search detects positive 4-rank with 240/582 agreement (41.24%).

- **PROVED.** On \(N\equiv1\bmod8\), target \(b=\mathbf1_{r_4\mathrm{Cl}(-4N)>0}=\mathbf1_{8\mid h(-4N)}\). The genus rank is two. On the ramified ideal classes above \(2,p\), the genus characters for \(-4,q^*\) give the sign matrix
  \[
  \begin{pmatrix}1&(-1/p)\\(2/q)&(p/q)\end{pmatrix}.
  \]
  Reading \(+1,-1\) as \(0,1\), this matrix is invertible exactly when \(p\equiv q\equiv3\bmod8\). Thus \(b=1-a_N(\Delta^9)\). [Rédei 4-rank formula, Thm. 3.1](https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.06250)
- **PROVED.** Equivalently, \(b=1\) iff \(N=x^2+y^2\) or \(N=x^2-2y^2\) over the integers. The two norm-Euclidean rings admit precisely the common prime classes \(\{1,5\}\) and \(\{1,7\}\bmod8\), respectively. The unit \(1+\sqrt2\) allows either norm sign.
- **PROVED.** The candidate outputs one iff \(N-y^2\) or \(N+2y^2\) is a square for some \(0\le y\le\lceil\log_2N\rceil\). It uses \(O(\log N)\) integer square tests. Every positive output is certified.
- **REFUTED.** The cutoff misses \(N=11009=101\cdot109\). Here \(h(-4N)=120\), and the smallest witness is \(11009=103^2+20^2\), beyond the cutoff \(14\). The failure persists for balanced inputs. Up to \(X\) the search can recognize only \(O(\sqrt X\log X)\) integers, by counting candidate pairs \((x,y)\). Balanced products of two primes \(5\bmod8\) alone supply \(\gg X/\log^2X\) positive inputs.
- **PROVED by exhaustive computation.** For every \(101\le p<q\le499\) with \(pq\equiv1\bmod8\), PARI `qfbclassno` gave **87 true positives, 153 true negatives, 342 false negatives, 0 false positives**. The unrestricted 4-rank criterion and the \(\Delta^9\) parity agreed on all 582 inputs. [Reproducible GP script](/Volumes/git/fc-redei/codex/scratch/redei_1_c/check.gp)